  "notes": [
    "Transcribed from T01-06/VSS96.dat in the Spada et al. (2011) archive. `source.transcribed_from` gives the file and its SHA-256. A row of that file gives the top radius of a layer and the properties of that layer, which extends down to the radius of the next row. The last row is the core.",
    "Figure 1 of Spada et al. (2011) draws the viscosity from T01-06/vis-vss96.dat, sampled at 5001 radii (script T01-06/visco.gmt). Every sample equals the viscosity of the layer it lies in, to 3e-16 relative, with a sample at an interface in the layer below it. Read with the radius as the bottom of a layer, the samples disagree. tests/test_vss96.py checks this and the transcription.",
    "The lithosphere is purely elastic. VSS96.dat writes its viscosity as 'infinity', stored here as null, as for M3-L70-V01. The profile of figure 1 writes it as 1e35 Pa s.",
    "VSS96.dat gives no gravity, so these layers carry no gravity_at_top_m_s2.",
    "Mantle layer 2 is 1 km thick, from 6151.5 to 6150.5 km, as in the file."
  ]
